CORE
main.cpp
--unwind 5 --pointer-check
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] .* copy owns its storage: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] .* source intact: SUCCESS$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
double delete: FAILURE$
--
Copying a std::vector copies each element by its copy constructor
([uninitialized.copy]/1).  The model of libstdc++'s __uninitialized_copy_a
copied the bytes also for elements that are not trivially copyable, so the
copy shared the source's storage, which was then deallocated twice.
